+## Kubernetes
+
+Kubernetes runs tools in a pod on a cluster you operate: one pod per run, created in the server's inferred namespace and driven through the Kubernetes exec API. Connection follows kube-standard inference — the in-cluster ServiceAccount when Fabro itself runs in the cluster, then `KUBECONFIG`, then `~/.kube/config`. There are no Fabro-specific Kubernetes settings beyond `server.sandbox.providers.kubernetes.enabled`.
+
+```toml title="workflow.toml"
+[environments.kube]
+provider = "kubernetes"
+
+[environments.kube.image]
+docker = "buildpack-deps:noble"
+
+[environments.kube.resources]
+cpu = 2
+memory = "4GB"
+disk = "20GB"
+
+[environments.kube.network]
+mode = "block"
+```
+
+Kubernetes is clone-based with the same contract as Docker and Daytona. The image must provide `/bin/bash` (the built-in default is `buildpack-deps:noble`); `image.dockerfile` is rejected — the provider runs prebuilt image references only, so pull secrets come from the namespace's ServiceAccount `imagePullSecrets`. `resources.disk` maps to the pod's `ephemeral-storage` request/limit, and user `labels` are applied to the pod alongside Fabro's managed labels. `lifecycle.auto_stop` is ignored, and `stop` deletes the pod: a resumed run recreates it from the checkpoint.
+
+`allow_all` creates no network policy, `block` creates a per-pod NetworkPolicy with no egress, and `cidr_allow_list` allows the listed CIDRs plus cluster DNS (port 53). Policy enforcement requires a CNI that supports `NetworkPolicy`. Kubernetes environments do not offer embedded terminals, preview URLs, or an install-wizard flow.
